  "query_preview": "..Not much change over the coming week. A strong high\npressure system aloft will remain centered over New Mexico and\nColorado through Wednesday then slowly shift westward becoming\ncentered over Arizona and Utah by next Saturday. As a result, it\nwill remain hot with low to medium level monsoon activity each\nday.\n\nFor the rest of today and tonight...Monsoon moisture remains in\nplace but on the somewhat low side, consistent with the past few\ndays. Instability is decent across the area but much like the\nmoisture, on the low side. In addition, weak steering flow aloft \nsuggests only a slight chance for storm organization with a \npreponderance of up and down storms with little movement. As a \nresult, we are expecting heavy downpours with 1.0-2.0 inches of \nrainfall in isolated spots. Most of us will see much less or even \nnothing at all. Look for scattered afternoon thunderstorms in the \nmountain areas such the Mogollon Rim, White Mountains, Chuska \nMountains, Black Mesa and the Kaibab plateau. Activity will then \nspread into the lower elevations, mainly isolated, but lingering \ninto the evening. After midnight any showers/storms will be few an\nfar between. Otherwise, hot with light winds. An Extreme Heat \nWarning remains in effect until 9:00 PM lower elevations of \nYavapai County and through 8:00 PM Sunday for the elevations below\n4000 feet from the Grand Canyon to northeastward to Page.\n\nOn Sunday...A virtual repeat of Saturday. Hot with light winds \nand thunderstorms first developing over the mountains late morning\nthen moving into the low elevations from the afternoon into the \nevening. Shower and thunderstorm threat coming to an end by \nmidnight. Extreme Heat Warning will continue below 4000 feet Grand\nCanyon to Page.\n\nFrom Monday onward...Monsoon moisture isn't going anywhere. Look\nfor isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms each day. The\nmountains will tend to see the first activity by late in the\nmorning where storms will be scattered. Later in the afternoon\nthunderstorms will move into the valleys and generally be more\nisolated. The northeast flow suggests weak and isolated storms\nnorth of the Mogollon Rim but south of the rim, as storms roll \ninto deeper moisture, a better chance of local flash flooding and\nsevere storms. Additional Extreme Heat Warnings are likely under \nstrong high pressure.",
